[sliver] Unavoidable fitted catch faces: each 1.6 mm strip is the bearing face of a catch window in a capped rib, `carb-1`'s under the ceiling or `fluid-28`'s on the wall, as deep as the window: the anchor cap lip's 1.2 mm engagement with a slip and the bridge's 0.25 mm finish allowance past the lip's tip.
alternatives: A deeper window narrows the rib's web for bearing the 1.2 mm lip never uses, and a shallower one lets the lip bottom out. A deeper engagement spreads the cap's wings past the faucet display cover's strain on the same estimate.
file: hardware/printed-parts/enclosure/enclosure/enclosure-back-top.step
click: x=52.350 y=276.125 z=339.022
click: x=-84.992 y=282.000 z=276.099

[ceiling] Unavoidable fitted catch bridges on a wall: `fluid-28`'s capped rib roots on a wall with its tube level, so the flank that looks print-down and the roof of the catch window cut in it are flat faces over air. Each is a bridge between the rib's two 3 mm end webs, which stand on the tied rib's 45° corbels (`tube_anchor_corbel_depth`); the window is the closed pocket the anchor cap's lip enters.
alternatives: A corbel under the flank fills the window and the wing's travel. Sloping the flank or the window's roof turns the lip's pull into a spread that walks the wing out of the window.
file: hardware/printed-parts/enclosure/enclosure/enclosure-back-top.step
click: x=-91.746 y=282.000 z=275.299
click: x=-84.685 y=282.000 z=276.899

[slope] Required keystone corbel turn-back: between the corbel's wall-grounded side cheeks the strip turns back at 45 degrees onto the DATA pad's run-out, keeping 1.0 mm off the soda union's clamp-nut envelope and 3.9 mm off the union itself; its lowest printed edge is a 15.56 mm bridge between the cheeks and both faces carry themselves.
alternatives: Running the strip on up the wall as the cheeks do enters the nut's envelope; filling it enters the union clearance.
file: hardware/printed-parts/enclosure/enclosure/enclosure-back-top.step
click: x=-40.710 y=463.334 z=322.840

[step] Wrong step classification: the two planes are facets of one face, the 45° strip that runs from back-top's Y465.30 bulkhead-row wall face along x −20.18..13.65 at Z321, which the solid carries as a single B-spline surface of 38.46 mm² rather than as a plane. Its facets' plane offsets scatter by up to 0.06 mm, and the comparison groups them as two planes 0.043 mm apart; no edge separates them. Reported to Derek.
alternatives: Group facets by the B-rep face they came from before comparing planes, so one face cannot read as two. Drawing the strip as an exact plane removes the scatter; the feature that makes it a B-spline is not yet identified.
file: hardware/printed-parts/enclosure/enclosure/enclosure-back-top.step
click: x=-3.262 y=465.378 z=321.095

[sliver] Specific fitted keystone swing ease: the strip is the square shoulder that ends the jack's 50° swing ease 1.0 mm over the aperture's top edge (`keystone_ease_shoulder`); 0.84 mm is that slope's run across the shoulder's 1.0 mm. The ease gives the purchased jack's top the room it swings through going in, and retention stays on the back catches.
alternatives: Running the ease out on the aperture's edge meets the face in a knife edge, and a taller shoulder leaves more open face over the jack.
file: hardware/printed-parts/enclosure/enclosure/enclosure-back-top.step
click: x=-40.710 y=467.370 z=311.552

[thin] Specific fitted keystone swing ease: the face's lip over the keystone's swing ease thickens up the ease's 50° slope from 0.84 mm where its square shoulder meets the face (`keystone_ease_shoulder`, 1.0 mm over the aperture's top edge); the lint reads it 1.36 mm through, up the slope.
alternatives: Running the ease out on the aperture's edge meets the face in a knife edge, and a taller shoulder leaves more open face over the jack.
file: hardware/printed-parts/enclosure/enclosure/enclosure-back-top.step
click: x=-37.063 y=467.790 z=312.674

[thin] Approved by Derek as drawn: each reading is the wall a flute groove leaves on a 3 mm section. The 1.2 mm groove (`flute_depth`) leaves 1.8 mm between its floor and the inner face (enclosure README, Nothing may relieve into the outermost 3 mm of a fluted face): on back-top's two flanks, and over the blind ends of the C14's two insert pilots, which stop one 3 mm wall short of the back face. Derek, 2026-10-21: "The flute grooves on 3mm walls is good with me and approved by me."
alternatives: A 4.2 mm wall keeps 3 mm under every groove and takes 1.2 mm more of the room inside each flank; a shallower groove changes the texture the corner coupon set.
file: hardware/printed-parts/enclosure/enclosure/enclosure-back-top.step
